# K_L is demanded only for a retained CONDITION_BOUNDED loop: CommunicationCatalogPlanner
# raises BOUNDED_LOOP_BODY_BOUND_UNDECLARED at `if (!fixedCount && declaredBound == 0)`,
# and an undeclared loop then runs at the inherited ceiling
# LoopBoundPolicy.MAX_LOOP_BODY_ITERATIONS = 8 rather than being refused.
# The three key-plumbing loops below it does NOT cover are all
# FIXED_COUNT_INDEXED (authority STATIC_ARRAY_INDEXED_FOR_V1), i.e. their trip
# count is the array length and is exact, so they must stay undeclared:
#   <...BlockTokenSecretManager: void addKeys(ExportedBlockKeys,boolean)>#u19
#     -- the allKeys PUT loop, receivedKeys.length
#   <...PBHelper: ...ExportedBlockKeysProto convert(ExportedBlockKeys)>#u14
#     -- the producer half of block-key-id/-expiry-date
#   <...ExportedBlockKeys: void readFields(DataInput)>#u16
# Declaring one of them would replace an exact count with a stated prefix.
# exportAllKeys() has no CFG loop at all (values().toArray is a library call).
# Trimmed from hdfs-17899's file for this case's slice.  A declared bound that
# names no RETAINED loop is a HARD refusal -- DeclaredLoopBodyBounds
# .refuseUnclaimed, BOUNDED_LOOP_BODY_BOUND_SITE_UNDECLARED -- while a retained
# loop with no bound is only a DIAGNOSTIC and runs at causynth_loop_default_bound.
# So err towards declaring less.  The Balancer's KeyManager$BlockKeyUpdater.run
# loop stays dropped: no path of this case enters it.
#
# The two Client$Connection.sendRpcRequest loops are DECLARED again as of
# 2026-09-22.  They were dropped with the reason "this workload never drives
# them", which was false the moment the symbolized RPC failure came back: the
# marker is minted INSIDE sendRpcRequest, and every path of this case drives
# IPC -- three DataNode heartbeats and the NameNode rotations.  Undeclared,
# they are retained-but-unbounded, so they only raise a DIAGNOSTIC and run at
# the inherited ceiling, which leaves the marker loosely bound to any one
# send.  hdfs-17899 bug3, which passes clean, declares exactly these two.
